//! Memory: a range of physical memory, and the one op it owns (spec §2.5).
//!
//! It imports the floor and no other `sos` module. `map` is not here, because
//! it needs a `Process`, and `Process` is declared in `sos.system`, which
//! imports this file. See `Process.map` there for the shape that cycle forces.
//!
//! A region is authority over bytes, not a description of them: there is no way
//! to read a Memory's base or length. `map` names a target and answers with a
//! capability rather than a number.

import sosabi.{SosStatus, MemoryHandle, MINT_OP, NO_HANDLE}

import sos.floor.{ALL_RIGHTS, call_value, sos_handle_release, handle_rights}

/// A handle to a Memory: an allocation, a sealed range of physical memory.
///
/// §2.5's RAM in use. A process allocates one out of a `Pool`, or receives one
/// at boot through `Process.boot_handle_next` (a child's image blob and its
/// destination are allocations), installs it with `Process.map`, passes it to
/// `System.process_create` as an image or a destination, gives it and donates
/// it. It is never cut: it is used whole and it goes back whole.
///
/// There is no way to read the bounds. What a caller names is a target
/// (`map`), and what it gets back is another capability.
///
/// Dropping the last handle gives back the slot and the bytes: the release
/// that takes a region's last reference to zero returns its kernel slab slot,
/// credits its creator's Memory quota row, and returns its whole range to the
/// free list of the pool it was allocated from, where it coalesces with its
/// free neighbours and, if it touches the unspent frontier, folds back into
/// it. An allocation minted at boot came from no pool, and its bytes go
/// nowhere. A range that needs a free-list node when none is left is dropped
/// and counted, never lost silently.
///
/// The last reference is not always the last handle. §2.5 frees a region when
/// the last reference of either kind drops, and an installed row is the other
/// kind: a region that is still mapped somewhere, or that a child's image
/// rows grant, keeps its bytes after every handle onto it is gone, and they
/// come home at the last `unmap` (or at the death of the process whose domain
/// holds the row). Dropping a region you have mapped into a child is
/// therefore safe.
///
/// Built by `Pool.allocate`, `Process.boot_handle_next`, `mint`, a
/// message's handle decode in `sos.pipe`, and a refused `System.slab_donate`
/// handing the region back: no word constructor, no word extractor.
///
/// `NoCopy`, and dropping it releases the handle. A move-only handle's drop
/// does something, which is the enforcement the discipline needs. See
/// `BootHandle` in `sos.system`: its `Memory` is an optional the caller takes,
/// not a field it would have to partially move out of.
public struct Memory {
    public(package) handle: MemoryHandle,
}

extension Memory: NoCopy {
    /// Drop is release; see `System`'s deinit for the sentinel and the discard.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        if self.handle != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.handle)
        }
    }
}

extension Memory {
    /// This handle's rights word, read from the kernel (`INFO_OP`), to test
    /// against `MemoryRight` bits. It is the handle's own word, so a sibling
    /// from `mint(rights:)` or a handle received with a keep mask reports the
    /// narrowed set. No right gates it, and it answers for a handle to an
    /// invalid object too, since it describes the handle and not the object. A
    /// live handle resolves, so the error channel carries only a kernel skew
    /// (`SosStatus.Unknown`).
    public func rights(&self) -> Result<UInt32, SosStatus> {
        handle_rights(self.handle)
    }

    /// Mint a sibling of this region handle, narrowed by a keep mask: the
    /// universal `MINT_OP`, on `System.mint`'s exact terms. Requires the
    /// universal `MemoryRight.Mint` on this handle.
    ///
    /// This is where a launcher's memory policy is written. A region's default
    /// set is permissive (`memory_rights()` carries `Transfer | Mint | Map |
    /// MapExecute`, because attenuation is monotonic and a bit not minted at
    /// boot could never appear later), so what a child may do with the RAM
    /// it is handed is decided here, once, by the mask the launcher writes:
    ///
    /// ```saw
    /// let for_child = pool_page.mint(rights: MemoryRight.Transfer
    ///                                        | MemoryRight.Map)!
    /// child.give(memory: move for_child, tag: RAM)
    /// ```
    ///
    /// That child can install its page and read and write it, and can never
    /// execute from it, not because the kernel asks who is calling but because
    /// nothing it holds carries `MapExecute`.
    ///
    /// The mask must keep `Transfer` if the sibling is to be given: `give`
    /// checks that bit on the handle being given. What arrives need not keep
    /// it, because `give`'s own keep mask narrows the child's entry (see
    /// `Process.give(memory:)`).
    ///
    /// Everything else `System.mint` says holds unchanged: the rights are this
    /// handle's intersected with the mask (so no-amplification is structural
    /// and naming an absent right is a no-op), `Mint` is itself maskable, and
    /// the sibling is independent (its own slot, generation and release) and is
    /// a second name onto one region, not a second region. A caller at its
    /// handle quota gets `SosStatus.QuotaExceeded` and a full handle table is
    /// `SosStatus.NoResource`; a handle without `Mint` terminates the caller.
    public func mint(&self,
                     rights: UInt32 = ALL_RIGHTS) -> Result<Memory, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, MINT_OP, rights as UInt, 0, 0)
        return Memory(handle: MemoryHandle(raw))
    }
}
